{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792940717,"nonce":"RzlFZWdC7ut8lpJcguZaQM6o2os=","title":"AOT executables are built without tree fusion, so binary_trees allocates 900 MB","message":"--build and --emit-aot were excluded from tree fusion in the CLI (\"export retains original bytecode\"), and emit_aot decodes the file itself, so a standalone executable ran the unfused program: bench_binary_trees as an AOT executable allocated 900.1 MB over 16 collections and took 307-366 ms, against 4.1 MB, 1 collection and 29 ms with fusion (hybrid 75 ms). On CI the Ash AOT row for binary_trees was 471 ms against the JVM's 211 ms.\n\nThe fusion contract is only valid for a fresh standalone process, so it stays off for --emit-aot (an object a host links), wasm builds, a build with a custom runtime, and any build with host links, exports, closures, object tails, drops or extra objects. prepare_standalone's own refusals (external native libraries, hosted modules, a runtime that is not linked in) still apply.\n\nChange: JITModule::new_aot_standalone runs prepare_standalone after the decode; emit_aot picks it for a one-command native --build; --no-tree-fusion turns it off through a process-wide switch. AotRequest and new_aot_with_host are unchanged, so embedders keep compiling.\n\nTest: crates/ash_cli/tests/tree_fusion.rs builds bench_binary_trees both ways and checks the checksum and the allocation (4.1 MB fused, 900 MB with --no-tree-fusion).","files":null}]}